# Point the loop at a real house. Copy to `.env` and fill in.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   .venv/bin/python -m flybrain.server
#
# `.env` is gitignored and is loaded automatically at startup, so there is nothing to
# `source` first. Real environment variables still win, so
# `FLYBRAIN_INTERVAL_S=5 .venv/bin/python -m flybrain.server` overrides the file.
#
# `HA_DRY_RUN=1` means a real Home Assistant is only ever *read*: the loop logs what it would
# have sent and changes nothing. Leave it on until you trust it.

HA_MODE=rest
HA_BASE_URL=http://homeassistant.local:8123
HA_TOKEN=replace-with-a-long-lived-access-token

# Read-only until you decide otherwise.
HA_DRY_RUN=1

# The entities to use. These have no sensible default — the built-in names belong to the
# *mock* home and will not exist in yours, and a missing thermometer makes the loop sit idle
# while looking configured.
FLYBRAIN_TEMPERATURE_ENTITY=sensor.your_room_temperature
FLYBRAIN_LIGHT_ENTITY=light.your_lamp

# Sensitivity: stretch a normal indoor swing onto the full trained colour range. A room that
# moves between 18 and 24 C should drive the whole range rather than barely shifting.
FLYBRAIN_SOURCE_MIN_C=18
FLYBRAIN_SOURCE_MAX_C=24

# The readout is trained across 2700-6500 K and LiveLoop clamps to the trained range, even if
# the lamp itself goes warmer. Reaching 2000 K means widening COLOUR_BANDS and re-fitting
# (~1 minute of GPU), not just changing this number.
FLYBRAIN_KELVIN_MIN=2700
FLYBRAIN_KELVIN_MAX=6500

# Only send when the colour has moved at least this much, so a real lamp is not spammed.
FLYBRAIN_DEADBAND_K=25

# --- optional ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Pacing. One decision costs ~2.3 s of GPU work, so this is the single biggest power knob.
# The default is already paced at 15 s (~40 W); set 0 for flat out (~165 W), which is what the
# demo wants. A paused loop idles near 20 W.
# FLYBRAIN_INTERVAL_S=15

# Adaptive pacing: while waiting for the heartbeat, the loop re-reads the sensors (which costs
# no GPU time), and if anything moves enough it runs at full rate for a burst - so the house
# being interesting buys detail while a quiet house stays cheap.
#
# The heartbeat above is still mandatory and is not redundancy: it is what keeps the reservoir
# in the regime the readout was fitted on, and it is the only source of *quiet* windows. A
# recording made only of events has no negatives for a `house_activity` readout to learn from.
#
# `FLYBRAIN_TRIGGER_DELTA` is in the primary sensor's own units - degrees Celsius here. 0 turns
# the trigger off and leaves a plain heartbeat.
# FLYBRAIN_POLL_S=5
# FLYBRAIN_BURST_S=10
# FLYBRAIN_TRIGGER_DELTA=0.3
#
# CAVEAT: pacing is part of the training regime. An event-weighted recording is a different
# distribution from a fixed-interval one, so a readout fitted under one pacing setting is not
# valid under another. Every recording states its pacing in meta.json for exactly this reason.

# Run even with no dashboard open. With a heartbeat this is what a house wants: the loop keeps
# sampling quietly and bursts when something happens, instead of running flat out forever.
# FLYBRAIN_ALWAYS_ON=1

# Record every window so new readouts can be trained on your own house later.
# FLYBRAIN_RECORD=1
# FLYBRAIN_RECORD_NAME=my-session

# Drive the brain from every sensory pathway discovery finds, not just the thermometer.
# CAVEAT: the colour readout was fitted with temperature alone driving the brain, and it reads
# the whole reservoir state. Extra drive changes that state, so the decoded colour is not
# trustworthy until the readout is re-fitted. Set to `off` for the trained behaviour.
# FLYBRAIN_CHANNELS=auto

# --- Jev: the optional judgment layer ------------------------------------------------------
#
# Jev is TypeSafe's "System One" decision model. It does not write prose: it returns typed
# answers from a set you supply. This project uses it as a teacher and an auditor for the
# dashboard — never as a narrator, and never in the render path. Design and TODO: docs/jev.md
#
# Called FIRST-PARTY at api.typesafe.ai, not through a reseller gateway. Measured interleaved on
# the same network, the first-party route was p50 313 ms / p90 423 ms against a gateway's
# p50 734 ms / p90 1739 ms — and Jev's `text->decisions` modality is not a drop-in on gateways
# built for chat models. Get a key at https://console.typesafe.ai/keys
#
# With no key set, the feature is off and the dashboard behaves exactly as it does today. That
# is the default, and there is no fallback that silently invents an answer.
#
# TYPESAFE_API_KEY=replace-with-a-typesafe-api-key
#
# NOTE: a prompt sends Home Assistant entity values to a third party. TypeSafe states Jev is not
# trained on customer requests or responses, but the data does leave this machine. Read the
# privacy section of docs/jev.md before turning this on in a real house.
#
# Pin the VERSIONED id, never the `jev-latest` alias. The alias moves when a release ships, which
# would silently invalidate any confidence threshold calibrated against it — and routing on
# confidence is the whole design. Note that the vendor's own SDK defaults to the alias.
# JEV_MODEL=jev-1.13.0
#
# One endpoint serves every model. Jev accepts neither `temperature` nor `max_tokens`.
# JEV_BASE_URL=https://api.typesafe.ai/v1/systemone
# JEV_TIMEOUT_S=30
#
# The measured TCP+TLS time to api.typesafe.ai, in milliseconds. Every latency figure is
# meaningless without it, and it must be measured *repeatedly*: the first sample on a machine is
# roughly 3x the warm median (154 ms vs 47 ms here), because of DNS and the initial handshake.
# Measure it with `python -c "from flybrain.jev import measure_network_floor_ms as m; print(m())"`.
# JEV_NETWORK_FLOOR_MS=47
#
# Confidence thresholds, per risk tier. A wrong on-screen verdict costs a glance; a wrong label
# silently corrupts the only training data there is; gating a Home Assistant action can change the
# house. So the floors rise with what the judgment is allowed to touch, and the defaults are set
# against the measured confidence gap (0.979 clear, 0.841 ambiguous): the clear case may write a
# label, the ambiguous one may only propose it.
#
# These MUST be re-calibrated on your own data before anything writes a label. An unusable value
# falls back to the default rather than loosening the gate.
# JEV_PAINT_ACT=0.70
# JEV_LABEL_ACT=0.95
# JEV_HA_ACT=0.98
#
# Refuse an answer whose reported model differs from the pinned id (default 1).
# JEV_STRICT_MODEL=1

